<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>KhaozEngine.Catalog.GameTypes</PackageId>
    <Version>$(KhaozEngineVersion)</Version>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <Description>The thirteen game-shaped content types most RPG-shaped worlds re-author from scratch (in Foundation, over KhaozEngine.Catalog): food, equip_profile and its stat lines, store and its shelves, monster_drop, gathering_node, recipe with its inputs and outputs, tool_tier, skill_curve and game_tuning. Each one is a stable type id in the game band, a stable key, an ordered field schema and a row codec, so two worlds that author the same facts store them the same way. A GAME DECLARES ITS TIME UNIT through ContentDurationUnit and the four duration fields take the matching name, with identical field order, kinds and codecs under either unit. The package owns no vocabulary of its own: a skill, a station, a repeat mode, an npc kind and a monster kind are all raw numbers a game gives meaning to, the finding codes are the engine's own KGT band, and there is no enum, no balance number and no roster anywhere in it. Pure .NET, no JSON, no external deps.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The positional *Index constants are internal on purpose: a position is not a consumer's to write
         down. The test project reaches them to pin each one against its schema's own IndexOf, which is what
         makes an off-by-one a failing test rather than a read of the neighbouring field. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="KhaozEngine.Catalog.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../KhaozEngine.Catalog/KhaozEngine.Catalog.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
